      "latency_ms": 18889.6,
      "prompt_tokens": 28,
      "completion_tokens": 1119,
      "output": "**Water** is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance that is the main constituent of Earth's hydrosphere and the fluids of all known living organisms. It is essential for all known forms of life.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is from chemical, physical, biological, and planetary perspectives:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Chemical Identity\n*   **Molecular Formula:** **H₂O** (two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom).\n*   **Molecular Structure:** The molecule is **bent (V-shaped)** at an angle of ~104.5°. Because oxygen is more electronegative than hydrogen, the molecule is **polar**—it has a partial negative charge near the oxygen and partial positive charges near the hydrogens.\n*   **Hydrogen Bonding:** This polarity allows water molecules to stick to each other via **hydrogen bonds**. This relatively weak but abundant intermolecular force is responsible for almost all of water’s unique properties.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Physical States (Phases)\nWater is one of the few substances that exists naturally on Earth in all three states:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Freezes at **0°C (32°F)** at standard pressure. Unusually, ice is **less dense** than liquid water (it expands ~9% upon freezing), causing it to float. This insulates bodies of water, allowing life to survive underneath.\n*   **Liquid (Water):** Exists between 0°C and 100°C. It has a very high **surface tension**, **specific heat capacity**, and **heat of vaporization**.\n*   **Gas (Water Vapor/Steam):** Boils at **100°C (212°F)** at standard pressure. It is a potent greenhouse gas.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key Anomalous Properties (Caused by Hydrogen Bonding)\nWater behaves differently than almost any other liquid:\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Its polarity allows it to dissolve more substances than any other liquid (salts, sugars, acids, bases, gases), facilitating chemical reactions and nutrient transport.\n*   **High Specific Heat Capacity:** It takes a lot of energy to raise water's temperature. This stabilizes Earth's climate and regulates body temperature in organisms.\n*   **High Heat of Vaporization:** Evaporation requires massive energy, making sweating/perspiration an extremely effective cooling mechanism.\n*   **Cohesion & Adhesion:** Water sticks to itself (cohesion) and other surfaces (adhesion). This creates **capillary action**, allowing water to move up plant roots and through tiny blood vessels.\n*   **Density Maximum at 4°C:** Liquid water is densest at 4°C. As it cools further toward freezing, it expands. This causes lakes to freeze from the top down.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Biological Role\n*   **Medium for Metabolism:** Almost all biochemical reactions (hydrolysis, condensation, photosynthesis, respiration) occur in aqueous solution.\n*   **Transport:** Blood (mostly water) transports oxygen, nutrients, hormones, and waste.\n*   **Structural Support:** Turgor pressure in plant cells and hydrostatic skeletons in worms/jellyfish rely on water incompressibility.\n*   **Temperature Regulation:** Homeostasis via sweating, panting, and blood flow redistribution.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Planetary Context (The Hydrosphere)\n*   **Coverage:** ~71% of Earth's surface.\n*   **Distribution:**\n    *   **97.5% Saline** (Oceans).\n    *   **2.5% Freshwater** (mostly locked in ice caps/glaciers ~68.7%, groundwater ~30.1%, surface water/lakes/rivers ~0.3%, atmosphere ~0.001%).\n*   **The Water Cycle (Hydrologic Cycle):** Continuous movement via evaporation, transpiration, condensation, precipitation, and runoff. This cycle distributes fresh water globally.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Human Use & Significance\n*   **Agriculture:** ~70% of global freshwater withdrawals (irrigation).\n*   **Industry:** ~19% (cooling, processing, manufacturing).\n*   **Domestic:** ~11% (drinking, sanitation, hygiene).\n*   **Energy:** Hydroelectric power; cooling for thermal/nuclear plants.\n*   **Scarcity:** Despite abundance, *accessible clean freshwater* is a finite, unevenly distributed resource under stress from pollution, climate change, and over-extraction.\n\n---\n\n### Summary Definition\n> **Water (H₂O) is a small, polar molecule whose hydrogen bonding network creates anomalous physical properties—most notably a solid phase that floats, a high capacity to store heat, and an unparalleled ability to dissolve substances. These properties make it the universal solvent for biology, the primary regulator of planetary climate, and the single most critical resource for human civilization.**"
